{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_01H2ovDKwgqzhU9AptnMzoKk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The numerical results are very informative. For the double-log with λ = -1/50, L₂ appears non-negative across a very wide sampling range (up to t,x ~ 10^14, c up to 5). The key improvement from N=20 to N=50 is that the R-dependent coefficient that flips sign at R_crit = A/B gets a much higher threshold, and the slower growth of R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(2/50) = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/25) means it takes astronomically large values to reach that threshold (if it even exists for N=50).\n\nBased on my analysis:\n- **Round 1**: ψ = -log(1+t) - √(x+1) failed conditions 1a, 3, sufficient\n- **Round 2**: ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) with λ=-1/20 passed conditions 1, 2, sufficient but **L₂ failed** at very large (1+t)(1+x)\n- **Key insight**: The same double-log ansatz with smaller |λ| = 1/50 appears to fix L₂\n\nLet me now write the candidate files."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":148967,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":148967,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53ae1852-07ed-492d-9643-b6f2f174213f","uuid":"4146b36d-ed9c-456b-be43-a0ee59cdf265"}
